  "explanation": "Two schedules are conflict equivalent if they involve the same set of operations and preserve the relative order of all conflicting operations.<br><br><strong>Conflicting pairs in the given schedule:</strong><br><div style=\"overflow-x: auto; max-width: 100%; margin: 12px 0;\"><table style=\"border-collapse: collapse; border: 1px solid #ffffff; text-align: center; width: 100%; font-size: 12px;\"><thead><tr style=\"border-bottom: 1px solid #ffffff;\"><th style=\"border: 1px solid #ffffff; padding: 4px 8px;\">Data Item</th><th style=\"border: 1px solid #ffffff; padding: 4px 8px;\">Conflicting Operations</th><th style=\"border: 1px solid #ffffff; padding: 4px 8px;\">Order in Given Schedule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style=\"border: 1px solid #ffffff; padding: 4px 8px;\">$B$</td><td style=\"border: 1px solid #ffffff; padding: 4px 8px;\">$T_2: R(B)$ and $T_1: W(B)$</td><td style=\"border: 1px solid #ffffff; padding: 4px 8px;\">$T_2 \\to T_1$</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border: 1px solid #ffffff; padding: 4px 8px;\">$B$</td><td style=\"border: 1px solid #ffffff; padding: 4px 8px;\">$T_2: W(B)$ and $T_1: W(B)$</td><td style=\"border: 1px solid #ffffff; padding: 4px 8px;\">$T_2 \\to T_1$</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border: 1px solid #ffffff; padding: 4px 8px;\">$C$</td><td style=\"border: 1px solid #ffffff; padding: 4px 8px;\">$T_1: R(C)$ and $T_2: W(C)$</td><td style=\"border: 1px solid #ffffff; padding: 4px 8px;\">$T_1 \\to T_2$</td></tr><tr><td style=\"border: 1px solid #ffffff; padding: 4px 8px;\">$D$</td><td style=\"border: 1px solid #ffffff; padding: 4px 8px;\">$T_2: R(D)$ and $T_1: W(D)$</td><td style=\"border: 1px solid #ffffff; padding: 4px 8px;\">$T_2 \\to T_1$</td></tr></tbody></table></div><br><strong>Evaluating Option (A):</strong><br>- $T_2 : R(B)$ and $T_2 : W(B)$ occur before $T_1 : W(B)$ ($T_2 \\to T_1$).<br>- $T_1 : R(C)$ occurs before $T_2 : W(C)$ ($T_1 \\to T_2$).<br>- $T_2 : R(D)$ occurs before $T_1 : W(D)$ ($T_2 \\to T_1$).<br><br>Option (A) maintains the exact relative order for all four conflicting pairs, making it conflict equivalent to the given schedule.",
